### Additional context

I tried to debug it as much as I could but couldn't figure out why, looked at mcp-go, modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk and the crush source code but I don't know why this happens. 
Interestingly, when using "bash" as a command and args `["-c", "<mcp-server> 2>/dev/null"]` it works fine. Also when wrapping it in a shell script containing `mcp-server "$@"`. 

It's not related to stderr directly though, even when the server doesn't write anything to stderr it breaks (even though stderr technically should be fine anyways). So maybe some file descriptor shenanigans or whatever, I couldn't figure it out :(

I generally observed that it always launches the mcp binary twice, first one exits 0, then the other one normally is used. But when broken, both of these show up as Zombie processes in btop for example.

