Currently when setting a config like:
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://charm.land/crush.json",
  "providers": {
    "anthropic": {"disable": true},
  }
}
```

models from anthropic still show up in the model picker. this add a bunch of visual noise that users can't easily opt out of.
It appears that this was not the intent (since `disable` is checked in the model picker and the simple resolution is to maintain disabled providers in the config, purely so their disabled-ness can be checked after initialization!

This PR has 3 features that relate to each other:

### 1. Limit file path walk depth/max items if the CWD is not a git repository

When it starts, it'll check if the CWD is a git directory, if it isn't, it'll limit the depth of `fsext` walks to `2` and max items to `100`.

If you run `crush` in your `$HOME`, for example, it'll likely never finish walking the tree. 
FZF has the same issue, but it "hides" it by doing things in background. This would require a bunch of extra work in Crush for it work properly, and will be made on another PR.

This is to "stop the bleed".

### 2. Allow the user to configure depth and max items

Allows the user to limit the FS walking stuff for both the `list` tool and for the `/` file completion:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://charm.land/crush.json",
  "tools": {
    "ls": {
      "max_depth": 3,
      "max_items": 100
    }
  },
  "options": {
    "tui": {
      "completions": {
        "max_depth": 5,
        "max_items": 1000
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This is the same configuration that is set in **item 1**, and will be respected over the git repository check.

### 3. Glob double star concurrency issues, tests

The Glob fast walk was not using a safe slice, which could cause race conditions.

Also fixed tests so they are simpler and more reliable.

this way users can simply list their lsps like so:

```json
{
  "lsp": {
    "gopls":{},
    "typescript-language-server": {}
  }
}
```

and it'll use the defaults :)

This PR adds pseudonymous usage metrics (tied to a device-specific hash), to inform development and support priorities. The metrics include solely usage metadata; prompts and responses are NEVER collected.

Metrics collection can be opted out by setting the environment variable by setting `export CRUSH_DISABLE_METRICS=1` in your environment, or by setting the following in your config:

```json
{
  "options": {
    "disable_metrics": true
  }
}
```

Crush also respects [`export DO_NOT_TRACK=1`](https://consoledonottrack.com/).

