# Feature: Environment Variable Configuration in Crush

This adds environment variable configuration support for Crush.
I felt the need for this when I had agent skills that required environment variables set and I wanted to have different configurations for them in different repos etc.

- Environment variables can be specified in `crush.json` under an `"env"` field 
- Variables are validated against POSIX standards (no starting with digits, no invalid chars)
- Variables are set during application startup before other components initialize
- Invalid environment variable names properly error with descriptive messages
- Empty env sections handled gracefully by defaulting to empty map

## Usage Example

Users can now add environment variables to their `crush.json`:

```json
{
  "env": {
    "MY_API_KEY": "secret123",
    "DATABASE_URL": "postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb"
  },
  "providers": {
    "openai": {
      "api_key": "$MY_API_KEY"
    }
  }
}
```

## Description

In `internal/pubsub/broker.go`, the `NewBrokerWithOptions` constructor accepts a `channelBufferSize` parameter but never stores or uses it. When `Subscribe()` creates new subscriber channels, it uses the hardcoded package constant `bufferSize = 64` instead of the value passed to the constructor.

## Root Cause

```go
// internal/pubsub/broker.go

const bufferSize = 64  // package-level constant

func NewBrokerWithOptions[T any](channelBufferSize, maxEvents int) *Broker[T] {
    return &Broker[T]{
        subs:      make(map[chan Event[T]]struct{}),
        done:      make(chan struct{}),
        maxEvents: maxEvents,
        // NOTE: channelBufferSize is NOT stored in the struct
    }
}

// In Subscribe():
func (b *Broker[T]) Subscribe() chan Event[T] {
    sub := make(chan Event[T], bufferSize)  // Uses the CONSTANT, not the constructor parameter
    // ...
}
```

Additionally, `maxEvents` IS stored in the struct but is **never read or enforced** anywhere in `Publish()` or `Subscribe()`. It is dead code.

## Impact

- Any caller passing a custom `channelBufferSize` to `NewBrokerWithOptions` receives a broker that silently ignores their configuration
- `maxEvents` is stored but never used — the event history grows unbounded regardless of the parameter
- Currently, all callers use `NewBroker()` which defaults to `(bufferSize, 1000)`, so there's no immediate runtime breakage — but the API is misleading

## Proposed Fix

Either:

**Option A**: Store and use the parameters:
```go
type Broker[T any] struct {
    // ...
    channelBufferSize int  // add field
    maxEvents         int
}

func (b *Broker[T]) Subscribe() chan Event[T] {
    sub := make(chan Event[T], b.channelBufferSize)  // use field
    // ...
}
```

**Option B**: Remove the misleading parameters and simplify the API:
```go
func NewBrokerWithOptions[T any](opts BrokerOptions) *Broker[T] { ... }
```

## Willingness to Contribute

Happy to submit a PR for either approach.

## Description

When using OpenCode/Crush with `google-vertex` provider, API requests fail with a 400 error when tool schemas contain `anyOf` fields. The Gemini/Vertex AI API has strict schema requirements that differ from standard JSON Schema.

## Error Message

```
Unable to submit request because `edit` functionDeclaration `parameters.edits` schema specified other fields alongside any_of. When using anyOf, it must be the only field set.
```

## Root Cause

The `processArrayItems()` function in `internal/llm/provider/gemini.go` does not handle `anyOf` in array items schema. When built-in tools (like `edit`) define complex parameter schemas with `anyOf`, the schema is passed directly to Vertex AI, which rejects it.

### Relevant Code

`internal/llm/provider/gemini.go`:

```go
func processArrayItems(paramMap map[string]interface{}) *genai.Schema {
	items, ok := paramMap["items"].(map[string]interface{})
	if !ok {
		return nil
	}
	return convertToSchema(items)
}
```

This function doesn't handle `anyOf`, `oneOf`, or `allOf` combinators.

## Environment

- **OpenCode version:** 1.2.10
- **Provider:** google-vertex
- **Model:** gemini-2.5-flash, gemini-3-pro-preview
- **OS:** macOS

## Suggested Fix

Handle `anyOf` by flattening to a merged object schema:

```go
func processArrayItems(paramMap map[string]interface{}) *genai.Schema {
	items, ok := paramMap["items"]
	if !ok {
		return nil
	}

	if itemsMap, ok := items.(map[string]interface{}); ok {
		if anyOf, ok := itemsMap["anyOf"].([]interface{}); ok && len(anyOf) > 0 {
			mergedProps := make(map[string]interface{})
			for _, variant := range anyOf {
				if variantMap, ok := variant.(map[string]interface{}); ok {
					if props, ok := variantMap["properties"].(map[string]interface{}); ok {
						for k, v := range props {
							mergedProps[k] = v
						}
					}
				}
			}
			if len(mergedProps) > 0 {
				return &genai.Schema{
					Type:       genai.TypeObject,
					Properties: convertSchemaProperties(mergedProps),
				}
			}
		}
		return convertToSchema(itemsMap)
	}
	return nil
}
```

## Related

- Similar fix in opencode-antigravity-auth: https://github.com/NoeFabris/opencode-antigravity-auth/pull/166

### Description

When crush is running `bazel build //...` the cache gets invalidated for some reason.
How can i configure crush to use my zsh environment to not trigger the bazel rebuild? 
It makes using crush impossible, because the bazel builds then takes ages out of crush

thanks, great tool btw!

**already tried this in `AGENTS.md`:**

```markdown
> **Important:** Please **always** run all commands with zsh and not bash to not invalidate the Bazel cache and use the zsh environment. If you are using bash, prefix all commands with `zsh -lic "<command>"` (e.g., `zsh -lic "bazel build //..."`).
```

**config:**

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://charm.land/crush.json",
  "lsp": {
    "go": {
      "command": "gopls"
    },
    "typescript": {
      "command": "tsgo",
      "args": ["--lsp", "--stdio"]
    }
  },
  "permissions": {
    "allowed_tools": ["zsh", "bash", "ls", "fzf", "fd", "rg", "cat", "bazel"]
  },
  "options": {
    "disabled_tools": ["git", "docker", "kubectl"],
    "disable_metrics": true
  }
}
```

**result:**
<img width="1111" height="835" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dfdeee07-f246-4750-a3f4-3cc1bc0af7cd" />

The main thing this PR does is add support for builtin skills via `go:embed`. It also includes a `crush-config` skill which can be used to configure Crush itself.

Builtin skills use a `/crush` path prefix. I was originally using `crush://` but it confused LLMs into thinking the paths were MCPs. If a user-level skill has the same name as a builtin skill, the one in user-lever will replace the builtin.

Also included here is a configuration option to disable skills, including builtins:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://charm.land/crush.json",
  "options": {
    "disabled_skills": ["crush-config"]
  }
}
```

